  "worked_solution": "From the fundamental theorem of calculus, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(4)-f(-4) \\\\ {\\int }_{-4}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(4)-(-1) \\\\ f(4) & =-1+{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, remember that the integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where we take the area as negative if the curve lies below the $x$-axis. So, using the labels on the graph, the integral is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x=8+(-5)+6=9.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the value of the integral, we can find $f(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =-1+{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+9 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
